   226|<section class="chapter" id="chapter-5"><h3>Chapter 5: The Spark Ignites</h3><img alt="Chapter 5 1960s TV repair shop pencil sketch generated with Gemini" class="chapter-image" src="data:image/png;base64,... [truncated]
   227|<p>The summer before tenth grade, Bob's father announced that it was time for his son to learn what work actually meant. Not the abstract work of school assignments, not the project work of the garage, but the clock-punching, customer-serving, money-earning work that occupied the majority of adult life. He had arranged a job for Bob at Hendricks TV &amp; Electronics, a small repair shop that smelled of solder flux and ozone and the particular mustiness of vacuum tubes past their prime. Mr. Hendricks was a bald man with perpetually cigarette-stained fingers and a habit of talking to himself while he worked. He took one look at the fifteen-year-old standing in his doorway and said, "Can you solder?"—"Yes, sir."—"Show me."</p>
   228|<p>Bob spent the next ten minutes demonstrating what he knew—how to tin an iron, how to heat the joint rather than the solder, how to create that bright, concave fillet that meant a good connection. Mr. Hendricks watched without comment, then handed him a basket of vacuum tubes. "Test these. The checker is in the back. Write down which ones are bad. When you're done, sweep the floor. You're hired. After that, we'll see about teaching you something."</p>
   229|<p>The job paid $1.25 an hour, minimum wage, but what it offered in education was worth far more. Bob spent that summer and the two that followed driving around town doing pickup and delivery with an older worker and learning the applied electronics that would form the foundation of his career. He learned to read schematic diagrams, those intricate maps of electron flow that turned abstract symbols into working circuits. He learned to trace signals through televisions and radios, to identify failure points by symptom, to repair rather than replace because customers couldn't afford new sets.</p>
   230|<h4>Thirty Thousand Volts</h4>
   231|<p>One hurried day at the shop, Bob cut between rows of television sets with their back panels off and their cords strewn across the floor like traps. He tripped, fell hard into a set, shattered the picture tube, and then—trying to catch himself—grabbed exactly the wrong thing. The power supply was still live. In that era a color television could hold a brutal charge of roughly thirty thousand volts, and the shock that hit him was enough to knock him unconscious for the better part of an hour. When he came to, he felt as if he had been beaten with hammers.</p>
   232|<p>It was a rough education in how casually danger had once been accepted around machines. Nobody held a seminar. Nobody wrote an incident report fit for the modern age. Bob simply learned what his body would remember forever: rushing and high voltage are natural enemies. The lesson stayed with him all through engineering, flight testing, and leadership. Safety is not theater. It is attention paid before consequence arrives.</p>
   233|<h4>The First Car</h4>
   234|<p>Bob's first car was a Chrysler Valiant. It was blue, and had a mind of its own. The heater only worked when it wanted to, which in Michigan meant I spent most of the winter driving with my windows cracked to keep the windshield from fogging up. The manual gear shift had a mind of its own, sometimes finding third when he was reaching for first, sometimes refusing to find any gear at all until I'd pumped the clutch three times.</p>
   235|<p>Bob's parents bought it with the arrangement that Bob would pay them back. Bob earned the money to buy it, working weekends at a local TV and radio repair shop. It represented something more than transportation—it represented independence, achievement, the first tangible evidence that Bob could provide for himself. Bob remembered driving to the lake with his friends, the wind in their hair, the radio blasting whatever station they could pick up. They had no destination in the strictest sense—they just drove. They followed roads they'd never taken, discovered places they'd never known existed, pulled over at random diners to eat french fries and just lived life.</p>
   236|<h4>The Curved Road</h4>
   237|<p>By 1971 Bob had acquired the kind of confidence that only youth can mistake for invulnerability. His daily drive to community college ran roughly twelve miles through countryside and along a lake road that curved as if it had been laid down by someone who valued beauty more than restraint. At first he drove it carefully. Then familiarity did what familiarity often does: it eroded caution. The road became challenge rather than scenery. Each trip invited a little more speed, a little more testing, a little more belief that skill could cancel risk.</p>
   238|<p>One day the lesson arrived wearing a sheriff's badge. Bob never saw the patrol car in time; by the time he realized he had company behind him, adrenaline had already made the decision before judgment could. He kept going. The chase ended only when he reached the college parking lot, where the sheriff pulled in behind him wearing the expression of a man who had just witnessed something equal parts impressive and foolish. He told Bob that he had been driving over seventy on a twisting two-lane country road and that it was crazy dangerous. He also admitted, with reluctant respect, that Bob must be one hell of a driver because he had struggled to keep him in sight. Then he wrote the ticket.</p>
   239|<p>It stayed with Bob because it was one of those rare moments when bravado meets its honest mirror. He had been lucky, not immortal. Skill did not erase physics; it merely postponed the bill. He never again drove at what he would later call crazy speed.</p>
   240|<h4>High School Engineering</h4>
   241|<p>Bob's high school didn't offer computer classes—they barely had functioning typewriters—but it did have a physics teacher named Mr. Kovacs who recognized something in the quiet student who sat in the front row and asked questions that went beyond the curriculum. "You're the TV shop kid, right?" "I work there, yes, sir."—"You ever think about science fair?" Bob hadn't. Science fair was for the rich kids, the ones who built volcanoes or grew mold in petri dishes. But Mr. Kovacs explained that engineering projects were different, that Bob could build something real, something that demonstrated understanding of principles.</p>
   242|<p>That winter, Bob designed and built an analog weather computer—not a digital machine like the IBM 1620, but a system of operational amplifiers and potentiometers that could solve differential equations. It was crude, limited, built from salvaged parts and vacuum tubes that glowed orange in the dim basement where he assembled it. But it worked. You could set up an equation, adjust the coefficients with dial switches, and watch the solution trace across an oscilloscope screen. The project won the regional science fair. More importantly, it won Bob a scholarship—a small one, not enough to cover everything, but enough to make college possible.</p>
   243|<h4>The IBM 1620</h4>
   244|<p>The first computer Bob ever used was an IBM 1620, one of the early scientific machines that helped define the 1960s. It had been introduced in 1959, and by the time Bob encountered it in high school it already looked like something from a colder, sterner future—large, expensive, utterly indifferent to human convenience. An IT man from the school district ran an after-school class where Bob and a few friends learned to program the district's machine in machine language, translating thought into binary instructions punched into cards they fed into the computer by hand.</p>
   245|<p>The machine's memory was tiny by modern standards, stored on a magnetic drum, but to Bob it felt almost supernatural. Here was a system that would do exactly what you told it and absolutely nothing you merely meant. There was no room for vagueness. Every instruction had to be right. Bob loved that severity. The class gave him a glimpse of a world in which logic could be built, tested, and made useful. The experience reached far beyond homework or novelty. It planted an idea that would steer decades of his life: computers were not gadgets. They were a new way of thinking made visible.</p>
   246|<h4>The Hallway</h4>
   247|<p>High school was not all circuits and books. One day a group of older boys with gang affiliations cornered Bob in a deserted hallway and demanded his money. He knew he was in real danger. He also knew he was not going to win on size or force. So he fought with the only weapon immediately available to him—his mind. He told them if they were going to knife him, they had better make it count, because the next time they turned their car key they might find the ignition wired to the gas tank. The bluff was outrageous, maybe reckless, but it worked. They backed away.</p>
   248|<p>Bob remembered the encounter not because it made him feel fearless, but because it taught him fear and action are not opposites. You can be frightened and still do the brave thing. That lesson echoed later in boardrooms, hospitals, airplanes, and every other place where composure mattered more than comfort.</p>
   249|<h4>Barb</h4>
   250|<p>In tenth grade there was Barb—funny, kind, and unpredictable in a way that made school feel less mechanical. Bob remembered her bringing dandelions into geometry class and insisting the teacher smell them, only to leave yellow dust on the woman's face and the room full of laughter. What stayed with him even longer than the prank was Barb's reaction afterward: she hated that she had hurt the teacher's feelings. Mischief without cruelty. That combination made an impression on him.</p>
